.TH CHROME 2
.SH NAME
Piece, Bevel, edges, panel, well, lamp, Gadget, gadget, put_pieces \- the
window chrome vocabulary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libdraw"
.PP
.nf
.B "Piece :: struct { x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, color: libpal.RGB }"
.B "Bevel :: enum { Raised, Recessed }"
.B "edges :: proc(out: []Piece, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, style: Bevel, light: libpal.RGB, dark: libpal.RGB, depth: int) -> int"
.B "panel :: proc(out: []Piece, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, style: Bevel, face: libpal.RGB, light: libpal.RGB, dark: libpal.RGB, depth: int) -> int"
.B "well :: proc(out: []Piece, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, depth := 2) -> int"
.B "lamp :: proc(out: []Piece, x: int, y: int, size: int, color: libpal.RGB, lit: bool) -> int"
.B "Gadget :: enum u8 { Close, Depth, Zoom, Size }"
.B "gadget :: proc(out: []Piece, x: int, y: int, size: int, kind: Gadget, pressed: bool) -> int"
.B "put_pieces :: proc(buf: []u8, at: int, id: u32, pieces: []Piece) -> int"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chrome
decomposes a bevelled control into a list of coloured rectangles, a
.BR Piece " each."
It paints nothing itself: the kernel's boot chassis walks the list onto a
framebuffer surface directly, and
.IR intuition (4)
walks the same list with
.B put_pieces
into a client's own command batch.
Both privilege levels read the same shapes because a
.B Piece's
colour is an
.B libpal.RGB
triple rather than a packed pixel word; each painter packs it for the
surface it draws on.
.PP
Every procedure here writes into
.I out
or
.I buf
starting at index or offset zero (or
.I at ,
for
.BR put_pieces )
and answers how many pieces it wrote, or how far the batch now reaches.
None of them allocates: a caller sizes its own array, at most
.B MAX_PIECES
for
.BR panel " and its callers, or " MAX_GADGET_PIECES
for
.BR gadget .
A box too small to hold an interior, or too small for the bevel depth
asked for, writes fewer pieces than the maximum, never past the end of
.IR out .
.PP
.B Edges
decomposes a bevelled border into its four sides, nested
.I depth
levels deep from the outside in, without a face: the top and left sides
take
.I light
under
.BR Raised " and " dark
under
.BR Recessed ,
and the bottom and right sides the other.
.B Panel
is
.B edges
with a face in front of it, one
.B Piece
the size of the whole box, drawn first so a painter that walks the list in
order gets the face before the edges land on top of it.
.PP
.B Well
is the specific recessed panel every sunken thing in this system uses: a
slate face with a lit magnesium edge below and a void edge above, two
levels deep by default.
.B Lamp
is a recessed socket of
.B MAGNESIUM_DARK
with a jewel inset: the jewel is
.I color
when
.I lit
is true, and
.I color
mixed toward
.B VOID
otherwise, so an unlit lamp still reads as the same lamp rather than a
neutral grey.
.PP
A
.B Gadget
is one of the four controls a window's title bar wears, and
.B gadget
draws one: a one-pixel raised (or, when
.I pressed
is true, recessed) square of magnesium from
.BR panel ,
with a glyph in amber on top.
.B Close
is a filled square, a
.B Depth
gadget two squares offset from each other,
.B Zoom
a hollow square, and
.B Size
two bars meeting at a corner.
Nothing here knows where a gadget sits on a frame or what pressing it
does; that placement is
.IR intuition (4)'s.
.PP
.B Put_pieces
is the one procedure here that knows there is a protocol: it walks
.I pieces
and writes one
.B put_fill
command, described in
.IR draw (2),
per piece into
.I buf
at
.IR at ,
skipping any with zero width or height.
It answers the new offset, or the offset it was given if the whole run
would not fit, the same convention
.B put_text
uses in
.IR draw (2);
a caller that gets its own offset back sends what it has and calls again.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libdraw/chrome.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR mui (2),
.IR intuition (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
None.
A box with no room for its bevel, or an
.I out
too short for what a call would write, answers however many pieces did
fit, including zero.
